From: Lovelace's Square <noreply@lovelacesquare.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:19:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Control channel: every request needs the window's key

The loopback control port took commands from anything that could reach
it: any local program, and a web page's fetch() to 127.0.0.1 could
smuggle a JSON line in a request body. This brings WordCraft's key
scheme (WordCraft 0.7.0, docs/control-protocol.md "Keys") to DeckCraft.

- control-key (new crate, shared by the app and the MCP bridge): the
  settings folder and the key file's name, control-key.local-<port>, or
  the Flatpak instance id inside Flatpak.
- deckcraft: once the port is bound, the window writes a random 256-bit
  key (getrandom) to <settings>/control-key.<instance> (mode 0600 on
  Unix), and removes it when it closes. Every request needs it, as the
  top-level `key` or once per connection with `auth`; it is compared in
  constant time, and a missing or wrong key gets "unauthorized" and the
  connection closed. A line that isn't JSON closes the connection (an
  HTTP request can't carry a command), a connection with no authorised
  request within 30 s is closed, and lines over 64 MiB are refused
  (pictures and media can come inline as base64). The settings folder
  now comes from the control-key crate (same paths as before).
- mcp: Remote (deckcraft-cli mcp --connect and deckcraft-cli app) sends
  the key from DECKCRAFT_CONTROL_KEY or the key file for the port, read
  again on every new connection, and only to loopback addresses.
- docs: control-protocol.md "Keys", mcp.md.

Tests ported from WordCraft: control_server (refused and closed without
or with a wrong key, auth once per connection, params.key, line limit,
first-request deadline, bad JSON, HTTP smuggling, key file life cycle,
stale key file), control-key, and the MCP bridge's key lookup and
loopback-only sending.

