 # Control protocol
 
-Start the app with `--control <port>`. The server listens on `127.0.0.1` only and speaks JSON
-lines: one request object per line, one reply per line.
+Start the app with `--control <port>` (or `DECKCRAFT_CONTROL_PORT=<port>`). The server listens on
+`127.0.0.1` only and speaks JSON lines: one request object per line, with the window's key (see
+[Keys](#keys)), one reply per line.
 
 ```json
-{"id": 1, "method": "engine.execute", "params": {"command": "shape.insert", "params": {"preset": "roundRect", "rect": [100, 100, 240, 120], "text": "Hello"}}}
+{"id": 1, "key": "…", "method": "engine.execute", "params": {"command": "shape.insert", "params": {"preset": "roundRect", "rect": [100, 100, 240, 120], "text": "Hello"}}}
 {"id": 1, "ok": true, "result": {"id": 258}}
 ```
 
+A line that isn't valid JSON gets one error reply, then the server closes the connection (so an
+HTTP request sent to the port can't run a command); reconnect to continue.
+
+## Keys
+
+Any local program (and possibly a web page) can reach a loopback port, so every request needs the
+window's key. Once the port is bound, the app writes a random key (256 bits, as hex) to
+`<settings>/control-key.<instance>` and removes the file when the window closes; a new start
+writes a new key. `<settings>` is the folder that holds the preferences: on Linux
+`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/deckcraft` (by default `~/.config/deckcraft`), on macOS
+`~/Library/Application Support/DeckCraft`, on Windows `%APPDATA%\DeckCraft`. `<instance>` is the
+Flatpak instance id inside Flatpak, and `local-<port>` otherwise. On Unix the file has mode 0600.
+
+```sh
+cat ~/.config/deckcraft/control-key.local-7979   # Linux, outside Flatpak, port 7979
+```
+
+- Send the key as top-level `"key"` with every request, or once per connection with
+  `{"method": "auth", "key": "…"}` (reply `{"ok": true, "result": {}}`); later requests on that
+  connection may leave `key` out. Only `auth` reads `params.key`; for other methods `params.key`
+  is an ordinary parameter (`ui.key {"key": "Enter"}` presses Enter).
+- The key is checked on every request. A missing or wrong key gets
+  `{"ok": false, "error": "unauthorized"}`, and the app closes the connection.
+- A line over 64 MiB gets `{"ok": false, "error": "line too long"}`, and the app closes the
+  connection.
+- A connection that sends no authorised request within 30 s of being accepted is closed. That is
+  one limit: blank lines and bad JSON do not extend it. After an authorised request the
+  connection may stay idle.
+
+`deckcraft-cli mcp --connect` and `deckcraft-cli app` take the key from `DECKCRAFT_CONTROL_KEY`,
+or else from the key file for the port they connect to, and read it again on every new
+connection (the app may have restarted). They send the key only to loopback addresses
+(`127.0.0.0/8`, `::1`, `localhost`). For any other host they send no key, say so, and the app
+refuses the requests: to drive an app on another machine, forward its port to `127.0.0.1` (for
+example `ssh -L 7979:127.0.0.1:7979 host`) and set `DECKCRAFT_CONTROL_KEY` to that app's key.
+
